  "notes": "{火葬|かそう} means cremation — the burning of a body after death. Composed of {火|か} (fire) + {葬|そう} (burial/funeral).\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {火葬|かそう}する: to cremate\n- {火葬場|かそうば}: crematorium\n- {火葬|かそう}{許可証|きょかしょう}: cremation permit\n- {火葬|かそう}に{付|ふ}す: to commit to cremation (formal)\n\nCULTURAL NOTE:\nJapan has one of the highest cremation rates in the world (over 99%). After cremation, family members use special chopsticks to pick bones from the ashes and place them in an urn ({骨壺|こつつぼ}). This ritual is called {骨上|こつあ}げ or お{骨拾|こつひろ}い.\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {火葬|かそう}: cremation\n- {土葬|どそう}: burial (rare in modern Japan)\n\nWATCH OUT:\nDo not confuse with {仮想|かそう} (virtual/imaginary) or {下層|かそう} (lower layer).",
